    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "With the invention of machine learning methods to learn any arbitrary function using the universal approximation theorem, there has been much curiosity around the application of such learning power to the human process of learning.\r\n\r\nIn this work, the main contributions are...\r\n- a framework to completely define the student learning problem,\r\n- a machine learning model that uses said framework to aid student learning,\r\n- an in-depth analysis of the framework's challenges and potential solutions,\r\n- discussion and development of data-efficient training methods, and\r\n- discussion and development of practical applications of this theory.\r\n\r\nThis paper calls such a framework ML-Assisted Student Learning (MLASL). Future work entails further research into the translation of information between vector spaces with large dimensionality differences, particularly in the large-to-small use case.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Celis, Diego. (2020). MLASL: ML-Assisted Student Learning. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: https://purl.stanford.edu/dn650wy9737",
            "type": "preferred citation",
